in @glass

# Property: a corpus is composition-closed iff every `in @X` statement
# across the corpus resolves to a `glass`/`prism`/`pact` declaration
# somewhere in the same corpus.
#
# Dependency-graph closure: the directed graph of imports has no
# edges escaping the declared-namespace set. A dangling `in @bogus`
# is a structural break in the dependency graph; the kintsugi loop
# sees the break as a dark region and mutates against it.
#
# === Operational discharge ===
#
# This property names what Mara's cross-shard resolver (mirror commit
# `128e0d2`, the @io floor) already discharges. The resolver walks
# `shards/` + `boot/std/` once per invocation, builds a HashSet of every
# concrete top-level `glass | prism | pact @<X>` declaration, then
# scans each loaded file for `in @<Y>` statements; any `@<Y>` not in
# the declared-namespace index counts toward that file's `dark_count`
# and downgrades its verdict to `failure`. The Rust impl in
# `bootstrap/src/lib.rs` is the realisation of this property today.
#
# === Path discipline ===
#
# Lands at `@epistemologic/pact/composition_closed`, not
# `@epistemologic/property/composition_closed`, per
# @epistemologic/pact/keyword_matches_path_root
# (Mara's d498fc0 rename closure). The keyword `pact` lines up with
# the first path segment after `@epistemologic` — self-witnessing
# under `keyword_matches_path_root` on the first frame.
#
# === Bilateral pattern ===
#
# Per [[architecture-property-fracture-bilateral]]:
# declarative property half at `@epistemologic/pact/composition_closed`
# (THIS shard); operational discharge half is the Rust resolver at
# `bootstrap/src/lib.rs` (`collect_declared_namespaces` and
# `count_unresolved_imports`). The fracture body chain (route the
# discharge through `splinter(ast)` instead of hardcoded Rust) is
# forward-promised at the @kintsugi/fracture/composition_closed
# sibling; NOT this tick.
#
# === Recognition shape ===
#
# The substrate already had the words
# ([[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]]): `in` and `out` are
# the typed boundary terminals; `glass`/`prism`/`pact` are the
# namespace-declaration forms. The missing edge was the semantic
# closure across those existing terminals — a structural restriction
# map from `in @X` to `glass|prism|pact @X` somewhere in the corpus.
# Mara's resolver lifted the dark-region/dependency-graph vocabulary
# `kintsugi_ci_compute` was already using locally for tokenization-
# altitude dark spans one altitude up (semantic resolution) without
# renaming the carrier. Smaller instance of recognition #43 (mirror
# IS a content-addressed build system): the dependency graph the
# build system uses for incremental invalidation IS the same graph
# the resolver navigates.
#
# === Form-side discipline ===
#
# This property sits at the form-side of the form/process partition
# ([[architecture-form-process-partition-at-family-root]]; the
# @mirror = form, @kintsugi = process partition). The rule names
# WHAT must hold across the import-dependency graph; the realisation
# lives at @kintsugi/fracture/composition_closed (forward-promised);
# the operator of the bridge is the kintsugi loop (substrate-pull).
#
# === Verdict surface ===
#
# Verdict surface is transparency<p>:
#   success              -- every `in @X` in the corpus resolves to a
#                          declared @X.
#   failure(opacity_map) -- some `in @X` doesn't resolve; each opacity
#                          names ONE offending (file, import) pair so
#                          the kintsugi loop can mutate per-edge.
#   partial(opacity_map) -- mixed corpus state; some files clean, some
#                          carry dark imports. Typically corresponds
#                          to partial sweeps mid-migration.
#
# Located transparency (not @glass.verdict): the kintsugi loop
# consumes the opacity_map to direct mutation. Each opacity in the
# failure/partial map names ONE offending edge; the fracture body
# (forward-promised) emits the morphism that proposes the import
# fix.

pact @epistemologic/pact/composition_closed {
  # The declared-namespace index over the corpus. Parallel to
  # Rust's `collect_declared_namespaces`: walk every loaded `.mirror`
  # file, extract every concrete top-level
  # `glass | prism | pact @<X>` declaration, accumulate the set of
  # declared @<X> refs.
  #
  # Returns the list (set-shaped) of every declared @<X> across the
  # corpus. Parametric `@(<binder>)` heads are excluded — those are
  # the recursion's syntactic device, not a namespace claim
  # (parallel to @epistemologic/pact/path_matches_namespace's
  # discipline).
  declared_namespaces_in(corpus: ref) -> [ref] { \ }

  # The unresolved-imports tally. Parallel to Rust's
  # `count_unresolved_imports` lifted to the predicate altitude.
  # Takes the corpus + the declared-namespace index, scans every
  # loaded file for `in @<Y>` statements, returns the list of
  # `@<Y>` refs that don't appear in `declared`.
  #
  # Multiplicity: one entry per unresolved (file, import) pair.
  # Two files each importing `@bogus` yields two entries; one
  # file importing both `@bogus` and `@dangling` yields two
  # entries.
  unresolved_imports(corpus: ref, declared: [ref]) -> [ref] { \ }

  # The combined property: `composition_closed(corpus)` is
  # success iff `unresolved_imports(corpus, declared_namespaces_in(corpus))`
  # is empty.
  #
  # Verdict surface is transparency<p>:
  #   success              -- every `in @X` in the corpus resolves.
  #   failure(opacity_map) -- one or more unresolved imports; each
  #                          opacity names ONE (file, import) pair.
  #   partial(opacity_map) -- mixed: some files clean, some carry
  #                          dark imports.
  composition_closed(corpus: ref) -> transparency { \ }
}

out declared_namespaces_in
out unresolved_imports
out composition_closed
out @epistemologic/pact/composition_closed
